A||||2||DJRice||Questions about adding a Cruise Control
Z||000000||DJRice||08-16-99||11:56 AM||||I have an 87 V6 auto that does not have a cruise control.  I have a junked out 85 that had a V6 with manual tranny that did have a cruise control.  <p>Is it likely that I will just be able to remove the needed components and swap them into my automatic?  My main concern is the wiring harness that runs from engine compartment to the dash.  Was this part wired for a cruise control that was just never installed?  Are the ECMs going to be different?  Do I need to find a donor car with an auto tranny?<p>
Z||000001||theogre||08-16-99||12:57 PM||||this will be a fun one........<p>if this was a 4 cyl I could tell you for sure that the ECM's don't interchange, nor would the cruise. Prior to 87 the cruise was controlled by a seperate module. In 87 the criuse functions where rolled into the ECM.<p>I think that still holds for the V6, but I can't say for sure. The V6, I'm told, used the same ECM for all years. If that's true, then the cruise should still be a seperate module, and portable from one car to another. Even if it's true, I'd anticipate allot of work, as I don't think the cars have the cabin wiring unless they were built with it. I think the cabin wire would have to be diferant depending on wether an 87 was a v6 or a 4.
Z||000002||DJRice||08-16-99||01:34 PM||||They both have the V6, but the one I drive is an 87 and the donor would be the 85.  I'll have to take a close look at the wiring.<p>I was hoping that the cruise install was going to be as easy as adding the chime unit was.
